Aim
To write and execute a Python program using Pandas and Matplotlib libraries to create different types of visualizations such as line charts, bar charts and histograms.
2. Algorithm
Start the program.
Import Pandas and Matplotlib libraries.
Create a dictionary containing student names, marks and study hours.
Convert the dictionary into a DataFrame using pd.DataFrame().
Display the DataFrame.
Create a line chart using plt.plot().
Add title, X-axis label, Y-axis label and grid.
Create a bar chart using plt.bar().
Display the values on the bars using plt.text().
Create a histogram using plt.hist() to show marks distribution.
Calculate the mean of marks using df['marks'].mean().
Display the mean value using a vertical line.
Display all the graphs using plt.show().
Stop the program.

source code:
import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

data = {
    'name': ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e'],
    'marks': [100, 70, None, 40, 50],
    'study_hours': [6, 2, 3, 1, 5]
}

df = pd.DataFrame(data)
print(df)

from matplotlib.lines import lineStyles

# line chart
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

plt.plot(df['name'], df['marks'], marker='D',
         linestyle='--', linewidth=3)
plt.title('student marks chart')
plt.xlabel('students')
plt.ylabel('marks')
plt.grid(True)
plt.show()

# bar chart
plt.bar(df['name'], df['marks'])

for i, val in enumerate(df['marks']):
    plt.text(i, val, str(val), ha='center', va='bottom')

plt.title('student marks chart')
plt.xlabel('students')
plt.ylabel('marks')
plt.ylim(0, 100)
plt.grid(axis='y')
plt.show()

# histogram
plt.hist(df['marks'], bins=5, edgecolor='black')
plt.title('marks distribution')
plt.xlabel('marks')
plt.ylabel('frequency')
plt.grid(True)
plt.show()

mean_value = df['marks'].mean()
plt.axvline(mean_value, color='r',
            linestyle='dashed', linewidth=2)
plt.show()

